    <DataNode elementId="AT2G37770-MONOMER" textLabel="aldo-keto reductase family 4 member C9" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q0PGJ6" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="335" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">ChlAKR / AKR4C9 is an NADPH-dependent aldo-keto reductase that can act on a wide range of substrates &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt;, including sugars, steroids, and aromatic and aliphatic aldehydes |CITS: [19616008]|. The enzyme could act both to reduce and oxidize compounds. For example, it reduced several carbonyls (C3, C17. C20) and oxidized hydroxyl group (C17) on ketosteroids |CITS: [19616008]|. When tested against saturated and unsaturated aldehydes and ketones in a separate study, it only showed activity with aldehydes |CITS: [21169366]|. Some representative reactions catalyzed by this enzyme &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; are shown, but, many other substrates have not been depicted. GFP-tagged AKR localizes to the chloroplast where where this enzyme likely helps to maintain the photosynthetic process by detoxifying reactive carbonyls formed during lipid peroxidation |CITS: [21169366]|. It may cooperate with other enzymes in that organelle, including |FRAME: AT1G23740| and the ChlADRs, such as |FRAME: AT1G54870|, which share some over-lapping substrates. In general, AKR might have higher efficiency for reducing the aldehyde group of most substrates compared to the ChlADRs |CITS: [21169366]|. Interestingly, &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; this protein is also able to reduce |FRAME: METHYL-GLYOXAL|, an &amp;alpha;-dicarbonyl compound that may be produced during glycolysis, fatty acid metabolism, and protein metabolism |CITS: [21169366]|. Transcript levels for this enzyme rise in response to a number of stresses, including osmotic and oxidative stress |CITS:[19616008]|. To date, the precise enzymatic reaction, or suite of reactions, catalyzed by AKR4C9 &lt;i&gt;in planta&lt;/i&gt; have not been determined, but, there is evidence that this enzyme plays a detoxifying role in periods of stress.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G37770-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT2G37770.2" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Interpro" value="IPR023210" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Panther" value="PTHR11732" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Smr" value="Q0PGJ6" />
