    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT3G04000-MONOMER" textLabel="aldehyde reductase (chloroplastic)" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9SQR2" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="415" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">ChlADR is an aldehyde reductase (ADR) that catalyzes the reduction of the carbonyl groups on saturated and alpha,beta-unsaturated aldehydes with 5 or more carbons &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt;. It is a member of the short-chain dehydrogenase/reductase (SDR) family of proteins |CITS: [21169366]|. The N-terminal region of this protein directs GFP to the chloroplast where where ChlADR likely helps to maintain the photosynthetic process by detoxifying reactive carbonyls formed during lipid peroxidation |CITS: [21169366]|. Interestingly, &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; this protein is also able to reduce |FRAME: CIS-3-HEXENAL|, a major plant volatile compound that contributes to green leaf odor, as well as |FRAME: METHYL-GLYOXAL|, an &amp;alpha;-dicarbonyl compound that is produced during glycolysis, fatty acid metabolism, and protein metabolism |CITS: [21169366]|. A second chloroplastic |FRAME: AT1G54870 ADR| as well as two cytosolic ADRs (|FRAME: AT2G24190| and |FRAME: AT3G61220|) in |FRAME: ORG-5993| with similar enzymatic activity have also been characterized |CITS: [21169366]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT3G04000-MONOMER" />
